.he "ide (4)                      9/24/26                      ide (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
ide
- an IDE hard disk on the MYIDE board
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
ide
device reads and writes an IDE (formerly AT attachment, ATA) hard
disk attached to an S100Computers MYIDE board.  The board carries an
8255 programmable peripheral interface between the S-100 bus and the
drive's own pins, at ports 0x30 through 0x34.  The drive's registers
are not mapped into the bus at all: the cpu puts a register number
and a strobe on one port, the byte on another, and moves a sector a
16-bit word at a time.  The cpu does all of it - the board carries no
dma controller.  The drive raises an interrupt when a command
finishes, and the driver sleeps on it, so the time a seek takes is
time the machine spends on something else.

The device number packs two things.  The major number is 4.  The low
bit of the minor number names which drive, and the bits above it name
the kind of controller, the way an HDDMA's name the drive's size;
this driver has one kind, which carries two drives.

.in +5
.nf
4/0  ide0       the board's first drive
4/1  ide1       the board's second drive
.in -5
.fi

Unlike an HDDMA drive, the size is not part of the name.  The
geometry comes off the disk, from the label that
.bd
mkfs
writes into the second half of the boot sector: a magic number, the
number of cylinders, heads and sectors, the rotation below, and the
size of the file system made on it.  The driver reads that label when
the device is opened, so there is no table of drive sizes to disagree
with it, and a disk carrying no label cannot be opened at all.

A block number is a file system block, and the driver turns it into a
cylinder and a sector.  Block 0 is not at cylinder 0: the volume is
rolled, so that the superblock and the inode list sit in the middle
of the platter rather than at its edge.  On an HDDMA the roll is
derived from the drive's size; here it is read from the label, so a
volume keeps whatever rotation it was made with.

The layout is otherwise the one the HDDMA uses, so a volume made for
either controller reads back block for block on the other.  The label
is what tells this driver that it can.

Booting from a drive on this card needs a monitor rom that knows the
card, which is MON 5.0.  The rom in a machine with an earlier monitor
boots a hard disk through the HDCA, and will not find this one, so a
machine that is to boot from an IDE drive needs that rom installed.

The card's interrupt is the bus line a slave Mult I/O board used for
its serial ports.  This driver takes that line, so a machine cannot
carry a slave Mult I/O board alongside this card.

The boot itself is the two level loader under
.bd
stand/boot:
a first level in the first sector of the boot cylinder, which the
rom reads and jumps to, and a second level, which the first level
reads from the sectors after it and enters.  A boot for an IDE drive
is not the same image as a boot for an HDDMA drive, because the two
drive different hardware;
.bd
mkfs -i
is handed the image when the volume is made.

A request is one 512 byte sector.  The driver issues the command and
sleeps until the drive interrupts for it; the sector's own 256 words
are then moved with interrupts off, because an interrupt taken
between the two port writes of a word would be taken for the drive's
answer and would advance the drive a word.  Every byte goes
through the cpu, so a transfer holds the machine for its length, and
an IDE drive cannot serve as the swap device: the kernel swaps a
whole 4096 byte page in one request, and this driver serves one 512
byte sector at a time and refuses the rest.  A machine that swaps
must keep its swap space on another disk.

A drive that does not answer a reset, a label that cannot be read,
and a label that is either not a Micronix label or names no geometry
all fail the open with ENXIO.  A request for a block past the end of
the volume fails the same way.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
